\p{Worked exercises accompanying [[0007]], my notebook on [[billingsleyProbabilityMeasure1995]]. The conceptual development — definitions, theorems, proofs — lives in the notebook; solved exercises live here, one chapter per section of the book. Every question is stated in full and every solution is folded away, so the page reads as a problem set until you ask it not to.}

\subtree[billingsley-s1]{
\title{Borel's Normal Number Theorem}
\meta{number}{1}

\exercise{1.1}{No infinite fair independent sequence on a discrete space}{
  \p{Show that a discrete probability space cannot carry an infinite sequence of independent events #{A_1, A_2, \ldots}, each of probability #{1/2}.}
}{
  \p{Denote the sample space #{\mathcal{X}}. Independence of #{A_1} and #{A_2} splits it into four cells of probability #{1/4} apiece:}
  ##{P(A_1 \cap A_2) + P(A_1 \cap A_2^c) + P(A_1^c \cap A_2) + P(A_1^c \cap A_2^c) = 1}
  \p{Every #{x \in \mathcal{X}} lies in exactly one of the four, so #{\mathbb{P}(\{x\}) \leq 1/4}. Running the same construction on #{A_1, \ldots, A_n} gives #{n} disjoint cells and the bound #{\mathbb{P}(\{x\}) \leq 1/n} for every #{x}; the sequence is infinite, so #{n} is unbounded and #{\mathbb{P}(\{x\}) = 0}.}
  \p{But the space is discrete, so all of its mass sits on its atoms:}
  ##{\sum_{x \in \mathcal{X}} \mathbb{P}(\{x\}) = 1}
  \p{A countable sum of zeros cannot be one, and we have our contradiction.}
}
}

\subtree[billingsley-s3]{
\title{Uniqueness}
\meta{number}{3}

\exercise{3.2}{Measurability as agreement of inner and outer measure}{
  \p{Let #{P} be a probability measure on a field #{\mathscr{F}_{0}}, define #{P^{\star}(A)} for every subset #{A} of #{\Omega} by [(3.1)](000T), and denote also by #{P} the extension of #{P} to #{\mathscr{F}=\sigma(\mathscr{F}_{0})}. Show that #{A} is [[000U]] if and only if #{P^*(A) = P_*(A)}.}
}{
  \p{Suppose #{P^*(A) = P_*(A)} and fix #{E \subset \Omega}. We must show}
  ##{P^*(E \cap A) + P^*(E \cap A^c) = P^*(E)}
  \p{The preceding part of the exercise supplies #{\overline{A}, \underline{A} \in \mathscr{F}} with #{\underline{A} \subset A \subset \overline{A}}, and the hypothesis collapses their measures onto each other:}
  ##{P(\overline{A}) = P^*(A) = P_*(A) = P(\underline{A})}
  \p{Approximate #{E} the same way — there is #{B \in \mathscr{F}} with #{E \subset B} and #{P^*(E) = P(B)}. Then}
  ##{P^*(E \cap A) \leq P^*(B \cap \overline{A}) = P(B \cap \overline{A})}
  \p{and}
  ##{P^*(B \cap A) \leq P^*(B \cap \underline{A}^c) = P(B \cap \underline{A}^c)}
  \p{so that}
  ##{P^*(E \cap A) + P^*(E \cap A^c) \leq P(B \cap \underline{A}^c) + P(B \cap \overline{A}) = P^*(E)}
  \p{as desired.}

  \remark{The difficulty is bookkeeping, not measure theory: three objects live on three different domains, and conflating them is what makes the problem look impenetrable.
  \ol{
    \li{#{P} is defined on the initial field #{\mathscr{F}_0}, the closure over finite unions and complements of #{\Omega}.}
    \li{#{P^*} is defined on the whole powerset #{\mathcal{P}(\Omega)}, but is \strong{not} a measure there. In particular #{E} need not lie in #{\mathscr{F}}, which is exactly why the argument has to route through #{B}.}
    \li{#{P} (abusing notation) is #{P^*} restricted to the [[000U]] subsets of #{\Omega}.}
  }
  The tempting shortcut — apply finite additivity and conclude everything is [[000U]] — fails because #{P^*} is countably subadditive only on the [[000U]] sets, not on #{\mathcal{P}(\Omega)}.}
}
}
